    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Bottom Line\n**Do not accept this offer as-is.** You should **negotiate aggressively**, and if Google refuses to move meaningfully, you should **walk away**.\n\nYou are in a position of maximum leverage: you were headhunted, you are happy in your current role, and you have a strong track record at Meta. Taking on the risk of switching teams and onboarding for what is essentially a lateral move and a negligible compensation bump (~5% in guaranteed Year 1 cash/equity) does not make financial or career sense.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Analysis of the Numbers\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Offered (Google L6) | Delta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Annual Equity (Initial)** | ~$250,000/yr | $270,000/yr* | **+$20,000** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | $0 |\n| **Estimated Year 1 Total Comp** | **$526,000** | **$552,000** | **+$26,000 (~5%)** |\n\n*\\*Note on Vesting:* If Google uses a front-loaded schedule (e.g., 33%/33%/22%/12%), Year 1 equity is higher (~$356k), but Years 3\u20134 drop sharply unless offset by heavy refreshes.\n\n#### Key Issues with the Current Offer\n1. **Base Salary is a Step Down:** The offered $235k is below your current base ($240k) and sits in the lower-middle of Google\u2019s stated band ($183k\u2013$271k).\n2. **Zero Sign-on Bonus:** Leaving mid-cycle forfeits near-term vesting momentum and bonus timing at Meta. Initial offers from Google almost always leave out the sign-on bonus as a negotiation buffer.\n3. **The \"Refreshes\" Trap:** Recruiter math on future stacking refreshes assumes standard performance, predictable stock performance, and company-wide grant policies remaining unchanged. You should **never price uncommitted refreshes into your baseline decision**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Your Negotiation Strategy & Specific Targets\n\nAt Google L6 in the Bay Area, strong/top-of-band offers for PMs reach **$650,000\u2013$720,000+** in Year 1 total compensation.\n\n#### What to Ask For:\n* **Base Salary:** **$260,000** (Pushes you closer to the $271k cap, ensuring a clear base increase over Meta).\n* **Initial RSUs:** **$1,350,000 / 4 years** (Up from $1.08M, representing ~$337.5k/yr).\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** (Compensates for leaving unvested equity and bridges Year 1 TC).\n\n*With these adjustments, Year 1 Total Comp would be approximately **$672,000\u2013$700,000**, which provides a legitimate 25\u201330% premium to justify the move.*\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Counter-Offer (Script)\n\nSchedule a call with your recruiter and keep the tone appreciative, firm, and anchored in your current value:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this package together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and I'm excited about the scope of the L6 role. However, looking at the total compensation package as it stands, it\u2019s essentially flat compared to what I\u2019m currently making at Meta when accounting for my base salary and upcoming vesting schedule.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum here, taking on the transition risk only makes sense if there\u2019s a compelling financial reason to move. To make this an easy 'yes' for me, we would need to be closer to:*\n>\n> * * **Base Salary:** $260,000*\n> * * **Equity:** $1.35M over 4 years*\n> * * **Sign-on Bonus:** $75,000 to offset the unvested equity I\u2019m walking away from*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to that level, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Recommendation\n* **If Google meets your counter (or settles near ~$620k+ TC + $50k sign-on):** Accept, as it provides a meaningful financial step-up alongside top-tier Big Tech brand diversification.\n* **If Google barely moves (e.g., bumps equity by only $50k and offers no sign-on):** **Walk away.** Stay at Meta, leverage your existing capital, and aim for promotion/comp growth on familiar ground.",
